\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 If you're a sucker for that "Pinocchio" sequence in which Geppetto gets swallowed by a whale, you'll dig Pulitzer Prize-winning novelist Daniel Kraus' "Whalefall," a drama about a Californian scuba diver stuck in the stomach of a 60-ton behemoth. That's bad enough. Worse, the kid's oxygen tank is running low. One part survival quest, one part ghost story, the narrative centers on the young man hallucinating his abusive dead father, who advises him on how to escape. I devoured the book in one weekend, but couldn't imagine how this claustrophobic nightmare could possibly translate to film. Director Brian Duffield ("Spontaneous," "No One Will Save You") has braved the challenge. Austin Abrams plays the trapped swimmer; Josh Brolin is his seafaring dad. It's a reunion for two of the leads in last year's "Weapons." Steel yourself for slimy bile, sticky emotions and wet tears. -- Amy Nicholson}
