\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Scottish actor Ian Charleson would forever be an Olympic runner on the beaches of "Chariots of Fire," head tipped back in a kind of spiritual ecstasy. The role brought him international acclaim, but there weren't enough of them, and a secret battle with HIV and AIDS took its toll. But what Charleson didn't know was that, near the end, he would embark on the run of his life, performing a legendary Hamlet onstage in a race with death. Andrew Scott carries the whole of this exhilarating biopic on his knitted brow, diving deeply into the metaphysical darkness of Shakespeare's text, into the rituals and camaraderie of actors and, ultimately, into the defiance of an artist who only wants to disappear once more into his art. Inexplicably overlooked for "All of Us Strangers" and "Blue Moon," Scott is just now arriving at a kind of effortless humility, dazzling yet approachable, that puts him in the company of the all-time greats. -- J. Rothkopf}
